# Boundaries

## Polyomino naming

Let's take a break from directedness and talk about polyomino naming. What should you call a polyomino? Some enthusiasts create unique naming schemas based on [chemical nomenclature](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9imS9K8uuZ0) or [polyhedra naming](https://www.seximal.net/polyomino), but the most common scheme for small minos is to name them based on a letter or shape that the mino looks like. In particular, Solomon Golomb came up with the following naming scheme that is commonly used:

![Golomb's pentomino names]({pentominoNames})

There isn't a common letter-naming scheme for hexominoes or above (you end up running out of letters pretty fast) but it's common to label all polyominoes consisting of a straight line of squares as I-minos:

{@render minoList(family_I, 16)}

Similarly, one could say that all polyominoes that form an L shape can be called a family of L-minos (or maybe V-minos if the lengths are the same).

{@render minoList(family_L, 16)}

By extension we can call minos that have an S shape S-minos (or maybe Z-minos):

{@render minoList(family_S, 16)}

Ones with a bar and a vertical line T-minos:

{@render minoList(family_T, 16)}

And ones with a single concavity U-minos:

{@render minoList(family_U, 16)}

While pondering the question of punctures, I ran across a different issue. I was starting to create pages for the directedness classes, and started to realize that there are big chunks of one class of polyomino. It would be nice if there was some more structure involved, if I could group each class into a further substructure, like polyomino graphs can be grouped into homeomorphism classes.

While I was staring at the different directedness classes, trying to make a further breakdown, I made a realization: the  polyominoes that belonged to the same "family" all seemed to belong to the same directedness class. "L" minos all seemed to be Ferrer's graphs. "S" and "W" minos all seemed to be staircases. "T"s were stacks, and "U"s were bar charts.

It seems like these directedness classes are pretty related to these "families" of polyominoes! But "looks like an L" is such a vague term, and we need to be mathematically precise. Does <MinoDiv mino={twoByFour} size={10} /> count as an I-shape? Is <MinoDiv mino={thickL} size={10} /> an L?

One naive attempt is to say that two polyominoes belong in the same family if you can squash or stretch their single line segments[^1]. This is how we organized the families in the above lists. This is a nice start, but it leaves polyominoes with 2x2s in their own solitary groups, which felt undesirable. Again, most minos above a certain size are going to have 2x2s, so you'll end up just getting a lot of families with only one member, which hardly counts a family.

**Definition** A _boundary word_ for a (punctureless) polyomino is a string of words taken from the alphabet `lurd` indicating the path the boundary of a polyomino goes in a counterclockwise direction. A _boundary family_ is the set of all polyominoes that share the same boundary word (for some reflection or rotation).[^3]

(TODO simple example)

Categorizing polyominoes by boundary words, we get all the groupings we desire: every I is a rectangle, every L is a Ferrers diagram. But there are some surprising ones:

<MinoDiv mino={Polyomino.fromString("001_111_101")} size={12} />
<MinoDiv mino={Polyomino.fromString("0111_1101")} size={12} />

These two polyominoes belong to the same family, which is fine! Often, creating formal definitions leads us to unexpected results. That's part of the fun of mathematics!

## Relating boundaries and directedness

Now that we have a formal definition of boundary words, we can now state our observation formally:

**Theorem** Every member of a boundary family belongs to the same directedness class.

We don't need to check every class. We just need to relate orthogonal and diagonal directedness to the boundary word.

**Lemma** A punctureless polyomino is `u`-directed iff its boundary word does not contain the string `urd`.

_Proof_ Assume that a polyomino $M$ is `u`-directed and that its boundary word contains `urd`. Then there is a cell $c_l$ to the left of the `u` and a cell $c_r$ to the right of the `d` segment. There is a hole $h$ right below the `r` segment that is not part of the polyomino.

(TODO diagram)

Since we assume $M$ is `u`-directed, there is a cell $c$ on the bottom row of the polyomino that has a path to both $c_l$ and $c_r$. Now consider the path from $c$ to $c_l$ that goes through the `r` segments to $c_r$ then back to $c$. This path defines a loop that surrounds $h$, contradicting our earlier assertion that $M$ is punctureless.

(TODO diagram showing this loop)

**Lemma** A punctureless polyomino is `ur`-directed iff its boundary word does not contain the string `rd`.

We can do a similar topological proof as above, as exemplified by this diagram:

(TODO diagram for diagonals)

Since each directedness class is a combination of directedness restrictions from different directions, we can apply the theorem to each orthogonal and diagonal direction that a mino is directed from.

## Regular Expressions and State Machines

Now that we've found this correspondence, we can ask if there is a way to define each directedness class in terms of boundary words instead of our (admitedly clunky) path-based definition.

We can do so using _regular expressions_. The more restrictive classes are pretty easy to find regular expressions for. A rectangle is just `ru ld`. A Ferrer's diagram is a repeating sequence of `ru` followed by `ld`, or in regex notation, `(ru)+ld`. A staircase is a set of `ru`s followed by a sequence of `ld`s (`(ru)+(ld)+`).

Once we get out of nonconvex territory though, things start to become cumbersome. It's more revealing to construct the possibilities as state machines. These are the "state machines" of all the different directedness classes:

<StateDiagramPicker />

You can verify that these state machines are correct by taking the "forbidden paths" for each class (e.g. for antlers it's `urd` and `rdl`) and checking that these are the arrows that do not appear. Note that for simplicity, these state machines are only for a particular orientation, and start at the bottom-most `ru`. An `dru` occurs only once in a directed mino, and we are cutting the boundary word off at that location.

Now that we've defined a relationship between boundary families and directedness, we can 

With the relationship between boundary families and directedness, we can now see why punctures are fit naturally within the directedness schema: a punctured polyomino is one with internal boundary words. 

## Dealing with Punctures

**Definition** An internal boundary word is a string from the alphabet `lrud` going counterclockwise surrounding each puncture of a polyomino.

Note that punctures may be _polykings_: squares joined by diagonals and not necessarily edges. For example, for the polyomino below, we count it as having one puncture with internal boundary word `ululdrdr`:

<MinoDiv mino={Polyomino.fromString("1110_1011_1101_0111")} size={16} />

We would like to be able to deal with punctured polyominoes the same way we did for unpunctured ones: that is, we want a definition for directedness that depends on outer and inner boundary words. It's not a straightforward relationship. For example, consider the following polyomino:

<MinoDiv mino={Polyomino.fromString("1111_1001_1011_1110")} size={16} />

This mino's external boundary word is `ruruld`, and it _looks_ like it should be a Ferrer's diagram (well, a Ferrer's diagram with a hole), but it's actually a staircase: the little notch cannot be reached from the top-left corner by going down and right.

To answer the question of how punctures relate to boundary words, we can try generating a bunch of minos of different classes and see what their punctures look like:

(some examples; maybe just sketch drawing?)

contains one puncture with boundary word `ru lu ld rd`, and not two punctures with boundary words `ru ld` each.

After some inspection, you might notice that for every class, it seems like the holes are also members of the same class, but rotated 180 degrees!

**Theorem** A punctured polyomino $M$ is `u`-directed iff its outer boundary word is `u`-directed and all its punctures are `d`-directed. In particular, its outer boundary word does not contain the string `urd` and none of its inner boundary words contain the string `dlu`.

_Proof_ Assume a punctured polyomino's outer boundary word contains `urd`. Then by the same argument as before, this implies that the `urd` is the boundary of a puncture. But this contradicts our assumption that it is part of the outer boundary word.

Assume there is an inner boundary word that contains `dlu`. Let $c \in M$ be a cell directly above the `l` segment. It is clear that $c$ is not in the bottom row of the polyomino, so it is not a root cell as defined by directedness. It is easy to see that it is impossible to reach $c$ from the root cell without a `d` move.

(TODO diagram, maybe more formal proof)

## Cleaning up the hierarchy

With this, we have a full classification system for polyominoes:

<HierarchyChart nodes={hierarchy3Nodes} edges={hierarchy3Edges} />

Note that we have renamed some classes with more fanciful names. Instead of "convex", we call $\text{Dir}^0_4$ "diamonds", since punctured members of that class are obviously not convex. A convex polyomino is an unpunctured diamond. Similarly, Ferrer's diagrams are unpunctured stacks, convex directed minos are unpunctured forks, and so on.

Let us remind ourselves of our criteria and see if this hierarchy fits the bill:

### 1. Interesting Spread

This is true up to octominoes. In fact, we won't have examples of any of the lower classes, or a completely undirected polyomino, until $n = 11$, which is not ideal, but at least we can provide the smallest examples of those kinds. In fact, directedness classes scale incredibly well. This is the distribution of classes up to $n = 16$:

{@render classTable()}

I conjecture that, like many other polyomino properties, the number of undirected polyominoes will eventually overtake directed ones, but for any reasonable size to display, they're still good.

### 2. Finiteness

There is a hierarchy of fourteen classes, each some valid combination of corner- and side-directedness. All possible classes are exhausted, and it is impossible to add a new one. Each class can be divided into boundary families, which do grow unbounded, but we can use fourteen colors for the main classes.

### 3. Intuitive

This one is hard to validate, but I hope it's pretty clear how (non-) directedness corresponds to having a concavity only on one side of a polyomino. To test whether any of this makes sense, I made a little puzzle including some polyomino properties (including symmetry and graphs) and tested it out with some friends:

(TODO minoan tesserae puzzle)

Through several group playthroughs, though people generally were not able to name the exact definition of "directedness", they were able to intuit the properties of concavities.

So with that, I have determined the final classification that I use in this website, which you can see over in the ["Classes" section](/classes). But of course, any good mathematical rabbit hole inspires a dozen more questions with every answer it provides, and I will talk more about that in the next section.


[^1]: I was inspired by [this puzzle](https://erich-friedman.github.io/mathmagic/0609.html) in Erich Friedman's Math Magic archive.

[^2]: In particular, it feels like a discrete version of [Green's Theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green%27s_theorem), relating information about areas to information about borders.

[^3]: This definition is inspired by a similar definition of boundaries used in [tiling criteria](/tiling).

